<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shams al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1021</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shams al-Dawla was the Buyid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and the surrounding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> from 997 to about 1021, a son of Fakhr al-Dawla. He governed at first alongside his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> at Ray, then independently, and fought the Kurdish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids">Hasanwayhids</a>. The philosopher and physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> served as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and physician.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Shams al-Dawla" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Shams_al-Dawla,_the_Buyid_ruler_of_Hamadan_and_Qirmisin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
